<header><p class="st-kicker">emirate · Arabia and the Gulf</p><h1 class="st-title">Jarwanid dynasty</h1><p class="st-meta">1305 CE – 1440 CE (approx.) · capital Qatif</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Shi'a dynasty that ruled Qatif and the Bahrain islands in the 14th and early 15th centuries under the shadow of Hormuz.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Jarwanids took control of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qatif">Qatif</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/sheikhdom-of-bahrain">Bahrain</a> islands in the early 14th century, displacing the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/usfurid-dynasty">Usfurid dynasty</a> there. They are usually described as an Arab family of eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a> and adherents of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/twelver-shiism">Twelver Shi'ism</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The dynasty governed the coastal towns and pearl fisheries and depended on the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-ormus">Kingdom of Ormus</a>, to which it paid tribute and whose kings sometimes intervened in its affairs. The traveller <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-battuta">Ibn Battuta</a>, visiting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qatif">Qatif</a> around 1331, described a town of Arab tribesmen who were committed Shi'a. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/twelver-shiism">Twelver</a> scholarship flourished in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/sheikhdom-of-bahrain">Bahrain</a> under their rule.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Around 1440 the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/jabrid-dynasty">Jabrid dynasty</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bedouin">Bedouin</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-ahsa-oasis">al-Ahsa</a>, conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qatif">Qatif</a> and the islands, ending Jarwanid rule.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/jarwanid-dynasty.jpg" alt="Jarwanid dynasty" width="320" height="271"><figcaption>Image: عبدالعزيز علي · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_Jarwanid_Emirate.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1305 CE – 1440 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>emirate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Qatif</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Twelver Shi'a Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Arabic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/saudi-arabia">Saudi Arabia</a>, <a href="/countries/bahrain">Bahrain</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Jarwanids, Banu Jarwan</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1372&amp;focus=jarwanid-dynasty" aria-label="Jarwanid dynasty on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5998 1202 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Jarwanid dynasty lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6318 1458L6485 1440"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6485 1440h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6318 1458h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6318 1458h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Qatif. <a href="/atlas?year=1372&amp;focus=jarwanid-dynasty">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
